b-salad"],["Cobbler (food)","dish/cobbler-food"],["Cook's Corner","dish/cook-s-corner"],["Crab cake","dish/crab-cake"],["Croffle","dish/croffle"],["Dagwood Bumstead","dish/dagwood-bumstead"],["Dagwood sandwich","dish/dagwood-sandwich"],["Deep-dish pizza","dish/deep-dish-pizza"],["Desperation pie","dish/desperation-pie"],["Dirty rice","dish/dirty-rice"],["Donut hole","dish/donut-hole"],["Egg in the basket","dish/egg-in-the-basket"],["Fish and chips","dish/fish-and-chips"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/f/f1/Lobster_Thermidor.jpg/500px-Lobster_Thermidor.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"thefoodplace.co.uk","lic":"CC BY 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lobster_Thermidor.jpg"}},"dish/oliebol":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/oliebol","n":"Oliebol","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Dutch cuisine","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["An oliebol is a Dutch beignet, a variety of doughnut that is traditionally eaten on New Year's Eve. People often eat it with raisins baked inside and with powdered sugar on top. Another variation is made with apple inside instead of raisins. There are similar foods all around the world, including the Samoan panikeke, eaten mostly with jam or butter on top."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-oliebol"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oliebol"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Dutch cuisine","cuisine/dutch-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":11,"links":[["Baking powder","ingredient/baking-powder"],["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Milk","ingredient/milk"],["Raisin","ingredient/raisin"],["Ribes","ingredient/ribes"],["Succade","ingredient/succade"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"],["Sultana (grape)","ingredient/sultana-grape"],["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"],["Yeast","ingredient/yeast"],["Zest","ingredient/zest"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"By Andrea Janssen","ref":"site/byandreajanssen-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"byandreajanssen.com","note":"Dutch food blogger writing in English about Dutch and everyday home recipes.","url":"https://www.byandreajanssen.com/about/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from Dutch cuisine","links":[["Boerenjongens","dish/boerenjongens"],["Lekkerbekje","dish/lekkerbekje"],["Pepernoot","dish/pepernoot"],["Speculaas","dish/speculaas"],["Stamppot","dish/stamppot"],["Uitsmijter","dish/uitsmijter"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/8/87/Oliebollen.jpg/500px-Oliebollen.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Teunie","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Oliebollen.jpg"}},"dish/sabich":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/sabich","n":"Sabich","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Israeli cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Sabich or sabih is a sandwich of pita or laffa bread stuffed with fried eggplant, hard-boiled eggs, chopped salad, parsley, amba and tahini sauce. It is a staple of Israeli cuisine and is based on a traditional Iraqi Jewish breakfast. It was created in Israel by Sabich Tzvi Halabi, an Iraqi Jewish immigrant, in the early 1960s, who put the ingredients, which were traditionally served separately, into a single pita."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-sabich"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sabich"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Israeli cuisine","cuisine/israeli-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":11,"links":[["Amba","ingredient/amba"],["Amba (condiment)","ingredient/amba-condiment"],["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Eggplant","ingredient/eggplant"],["Hummus","ingredient/hummus"],["Israeli salad","ingredient/israeli-salad"],["Laffa","ingredient/laffa"],["Parsley","ingredient/parsley"],["Pita","ingredient/pita"],["Salad","ingredient/salad"],["Tahini","ingredient/tahini"]]},{"h":"More from Israeli cuisine","links":[["Baba ghanoush","dish/baba-ghanoush"],["Cabbage roll","dish/cabbage-roll"],["Hummus","dish/hummus"],["Lahoh","dish/lahoh"],["Sufganiyah","dish/sufganiyah"],["Taboon bread","dish/taboon-bread"],["Ziva (dish)","dish/ziva-dish"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/f/f8/Sabich.jpg/500px-Sabich.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Gilabrand","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sabich.jpg"}},"ingredient/calabaza":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/calabaza","n":"Calabaza","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Calabaza is the generic name in the Spanish language for any type of winter squash. Within an English-language context it specifically refers to the West Indian pumpkin, a winter squash typically grown in the West Indies, tropical America, and the Philippines. Calabaza is the common name for Cucurbita moschata in Cuba, Florida, Puerto Rico, Mexico and the Philippines (where it is also spelled kalabasa). C. moschata is also known as ahuyama in Colombia, the Dominican Republic and Venezuela; ayote in Central America; zapallo in certain countries of South America; and \"pumpkin\", \"squash\", or \"calabash\" in English-speaking islands."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-calabaza"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Calabaza"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Haitian cuisine","cuisine/haitian-cuisine",[["Soup joumou","dish/soup-joumou"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Beef","ingredient/beef"],["Potato","ingredient/potato"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/4/4e/Kalabasa_%28Calabaza%29_squash_from_the_Philippines.jpg/500px-Kalabasa_%28Calabaza%29_squash_from_the_Philippines.jpg","w":500,"h":367,"by":"Obsidian Soul","lic":"CC0","licUrl":"http://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/deed.en","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kalabasa_(Calabaza)_squash_from_the_Philippines.jpg"}},"dish/windsor-soup":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/windsor-soup","n":"Windsor soup","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from English cuisine","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["Windsor soup or Brown Windsor soup is a British soup. While commonly associated with the Victorian and Edwardian eras, the practice of calling it 'Brown Windsor' did not emerge until at least the 1920s, and the name was usually associated with low-quality brown soup of uncertain ingredients. Although Windsor soup included elegant recipes among famous chefs of the 19th century, the 'Brown Windsor' varieties became an institutional gruel that gained a reputation as indicative of bad English food during the mid-20th century, and a later source of jokes, myths and legends."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-windsor-soup"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Windsor_soup"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["English cuisine","cuisine/english-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":6,"links":[["Bouquet garni","ingredient/bouquet-garni"],["Broth","ingredient/broth"],["Carrot","ingredient/carrot"],["Madeira wine","ingredient/madeira-wine"],["Noodles","ingredient/noodles"],["Rice","ingredient/rice"]]},{"h":"More from English cuisine","links":[["Angels on horseback","dish/angels-on-horseback"],["Cabinet pudding","dish/cabinet-pudding"],["Clotted cream","dish/clotted-cream"],["Crème anglaise","dish/creme-anglaise"],["Fish and chips","dish/fish-and-chips"],["Macaroni and cheese","dish/macaroni-and-cheese"],["Maids of honour tart","dish/maids-of-honour-tart"],["Manchester tart","dish/manchester-tart"],["Mock turtle soup","dish/mock-turtle-soup"],["Pie and mash","dish/pie-and-mash"],["Pork pie","dish/pork-pie"],["Pottage","dish/pottage"]]}]},"ingredient/guayusa":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/guayusa","n":"Guayusa","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 0 dishes across 0 cuisines","intro":[["Leaves of a native Amazon plant, brewed into a morning drink with about as much caffeine as coffee. ",["Kichwa","cuisine/amazonian-kichwa-foodways"]," entrepreneur Esthela Noteno bottles it in Ecuador from her grandmother Juana Andi's recipe."]],"src":"note","sources":[{"name":"World Wildlife Fund, In Ecuador, a natural beverage is rooted in Indigenous tradition","url":"https://www.worldwildlife.org/news/magazine/winter-2024/in-ecuador-a-natural-beverage-is-rooted-in-indigenous-tradition/"}],"actions":[],"sections":[{"h":"Mentioned in","links":[["Amazonian Kichwa foodways","cuisine/amazonian-kichwa-foodways"]]}]},"cookbook/the-taste-of-belgium":{"t":"book","r":"cookbook/the-taste-of-belgium","n":"The Taste of Belgium","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Cookbooks","cookbooks"]],"sub":"Cookbook by Ruth Van Waerebeek","square":true,"suggested":true,"intro":[["Belgian-born cook's well-known English-language collection of Belgian home recipes."]],"actions":[],"sections":[{"h":"Cuisines it covers","count":1,"links":[["Belgian cuisine","cuisine/belgian-cuisine"]]}]},"site/the-pasta-project-com":{"t":"site","r":"site/the-pasta-project-com","n":"The Pasta Project: Abruzzo","c":"#55b8ad","crumb":[["Recipe sites","sites"]],"sub":"Recipe site, the-pasta-project.com","square":true,"suggested":true,"intro":[["Italy-based pasta blog's section on regional pasta dishes from Abruzzo."]],"actions":[{"kind":"out","url":"https://the-pasta-project.com","label":"Visit the-pasta-project.com"}],"sections":[{"h":"Cuisines it covers","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Abruzzese and Molisan cuisine","ref":"cuisine/cuisine-of-abruzzo","color":"#cf5f87","shape":"cuisine","sub":"The Pasta Project: Abruzzo","url":"https://www.the-pasta-project.com/abruzzo/"}]}]}}